{"id":1068,"date":"2026-03-23T09:00:24","date_gmt":"2026-03-23T09:00:24","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:10005\/?p=1068"},"modified":"2026-03-23T09:00:24","modified_gmt":"2026-03-23T09:00:24","slug":"%e7%a0%b4%e5%9d%8f%e5%9f%ba%e4%ba%8e%e4%bf%a1%e9%81%93%e4%ba%92%e6%98%93%e6%80%a7%e7%9a%84%e5%af%86%e9%92%a5%e7%94%9f%e6%88%90%e4%b8%ad%e7%9a%84%e5%af%86%e9%92%a5%e4%b8%80%e8%87%b4%e6%80%a7","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/en\/%e7%a0%b4%e5%9d%8f%e5%9f%ba%e4%ba%8e%e4%bf%a1%e9%81%93%e4%ba%92%e6%98%93%e6%80%a7%e7%9a%84%e5%af%86%e9%92%a5%e7%94%9f%e6%88%90%e4%b8%ad%e7%9a%84%e5%af%86%e9%92%a5%e4%b8%80%e8%87%b4%e6%80%a7\/","title":{"rendered":"Breaking Key Agreement in Channel Reciprocity-Based Secret Key Generation"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3749991f wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"min-height:1000px\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-458a08b5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\"><h2 class=\"lab-paper-title wp-block-post-title\">Breaking Key Agreement in Channel Reciprocity-Based Secret Key Generation<\/h2>\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-paper-meta has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">LI G, STAAT P, LI H, et al.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-paper-meta has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><em>IEEE Transactions on Information Forensics and Security<\/em><\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons lab-btn-group is-layout-flex wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/RIS-Jamming_Breaking_Key_Consistency_in_Channel_Reciprocity-Based_Key_Generation.pdf\">\ud83d\udcc4 PDF<\/a><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-button is-style-fill\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/TIFS.2024.3389569\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/LWC.2023.3330809 noreferrer noopener\">\ud83d\udd17 DOI Link<\/a><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-text-color has-contrast-3-color has-alpha-channel-opacity has-contrast-3-background-color has-background is-style-wide\" style=\"margin-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-layout-flow wp-block-group-is-layout-flow\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-46005010 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udccc Abstract<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-pullquote has-text-align-left lab-abstract-quote has-small-font-size\" style=\"margin-right:var(--wp--preset--spacing--10);margin-left:var(--wp--preset--spacing--10);padding-top:0;padding-right:0;padding-bottom:0;padding-left:0\"><blockquote><p><a href=\"http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/RIS-Jamming_Breaking_Key_Consistency_in_Channel_Reciprocity-Based_Key_Generation.pdf\">Channel Reciprocity-based Key Generation (CRKG) exploits the reciprocal randomness of wireless channels to establish shared keys between wireless terminals, and is considered an important scheme to complement existing cryptographic techniques in future wireless networks. This paper proposes a novel attack\u2014the RIS-jamming attack\u2014and demonstrates that an attacker can disrupt channel reciprocity by deploying a malicious RIS, thereby preventing legitimate users from agreeing on the same key. Specifically, this paper elaborates on three implementation approaches for the RIS-jamming attack: employing active non-reciprocal circuits, performing asynchronous time-varying control, and reducing the signal-to-noise ratio. By establishing a quantitative relationship between the key rate and RIS deployment parameters, the attack effect is theoretically analyzed. To resist such attacks, this paper proposes a defense scheme based on multipath separation of wideband signals\u2014Contaminated Path Removal CRKG (CPR-CRKG)\u2014which identifies and removes malicious RIS paths, enabling legitimate users to still generate keys from the remaining paths. Simulation and experimental results corroborate each other: in a joint experiment with real Wi-Fi devices and a self-built RIS prototype, the attack increased the average bit disagreement rate (BDR) of the original key by 20%, while the proposed CPR-CRKG scheme can effectively mitigate the attack impact when the RIS propagation path is separable.<\/a><\/p><\/blockquote><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-6adec98e lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udca1 Core Contributions<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<ul start=\"1\" style=\"border-style:none;border-width:0px\" class=\"wp-block-list lab-feature-list\">\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Proposes the RIS-jamming attack and provides three implementation approaches<\/strong>: The attacker disrupts the channel reciprocity relied upon by CRKG by deploying a malicious RIS, with specific implementations including: \u2460 introducing active non-reciprocal circuits to make the uplink and downlink RIS reflection matrices inconsistent; \u2461 employing asynchronous time-varying configurations to randomly switch RIS phases within channel probing intervals; \u2462 increasing quantization errors by reducing the signal-to-noise ratio. Compared with traditional active jamming attacks, the RIS-jamming attack exploits the low cost and passive nature of RIS, requires no additional signal transmission, and is inherently immune to existing anti-jamming measures such as frequency hopping.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Establishes a quantitative relationship between the key rate and RIS deployment parameters, and theoretically analyzes the attack effect<\/strong>: Derives the analytical expressions for the key rate under RIS jamming <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">R_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\u3001<math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">R_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">R_3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>and the direct link gain <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>\u03b2<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\u2113<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\beta_\\ell<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>, RIS gain <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>\u03b2<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\beta_r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>, proving that the asynchronous RIS configuration attack (Attack 2) has stronger destructive power than the asymmetric RIS structure attack (Attack 1), and providing the theoretical boundary of the attack effect with respect to RIS deployment location (when the distance between Alice\/Bob and the RIS is 20% of the Alice-Bob spacing,<math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>\u03b2<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\beta_r &gt; 0.01<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>, the attack is feasible).<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Proposes the CPR-CRKG defense scheme, which removes contaminated paths using wideband multipath separation<\/strong>: Based on the time-domain resolution capability of OFDM wideband signals, an IDCT transform is applied to the channel estimation matrix to separate multipath components. The RIS-induced path is detected by comparing the autocorrelation coefficients of uplink and downlink paths and is removed from the available path set, thereby restoring key generation capability without relying on information reconciliation; the overall complexity is <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>K<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>log<\/mi><mo>\u2061<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">O(K^2 N_p + K \\log K + N_p N_{sel})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\u3002<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-2f9d57f4 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udd27 Method Overview<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group lab-paper-results is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-b46d2053 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-57-00.png ,http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-57-00.png 780w, http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-57-00.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-57-00.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1070\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-23_16-57-00\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<p class=\"lab-experiment-caption has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">The system consists of single-antenna Alice, Bob, and attacker Eve, employing a TDD-OFDM protocol (e.g., IEEE 802.11n Wi-Fi) for bidirectional channel probing. The effective channel is the superposition of the direct link and the RIS-induced link <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>h<\/mi><mo>~<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo separator=\"true\">,<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>\u03b2<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\u03a6<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><msub><mi>\u03d5<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\tilde{h}_{ba}(t, k) = h_d(k) + \\beta_r \\Phi(t) e^{j\\phi_r(t)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>, where the non-reciprocity of the RIS reflection matrix <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\u03a6<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\Phi(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> is the core of the attack.<strong>Attack phase<\/strong>, Eve makes the uplink and downlink <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\u03a6<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\Phi(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> inconsistent (Attack 1) or randomly switches within probing intervals (Attack 2), causing <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>h<\/mi><mo>~<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\tilde{h}_{ba}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>and <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>h<\/mi><mo>~<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\tilde{h}_{ab}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>to lose reciprocity, with the BDR rising to an error floor of 0.15~0.2.<strong>Defense phase<\/strong>, CPR-CRKG exploits the time-domain resolution of wideband OFDM, mapping the frequency-domain channel estimation matrix <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>H<\/mi><mo>~<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\tilde{H}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> to the time-domain path domain <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>G<\/mi><mo>~<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\tilde{G}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>via IDCT, computing the autocorrelation coefficient for each path to detect the rapidly varying RIS path, and after negotiated exclusion, performs quantization and key extraction only on the remaining paths.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-dda34f26 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udcca Experimental Results<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group lab-paper-results is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-080de2b0 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-58-06.png ,http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-58-06.png 780w, http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-58-06.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-58-06.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1071\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-23_16-58-06\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-080de2b0 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-58-17.png ,http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-58-17.png 780w, http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-58-17.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-58-17.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1072\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-23_16-58-17\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<p class=\"lab-experiment-caption has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>In a joint experiment with real Wi-Fi and a self-built RIS prototype, the RIS-jamming attack increased the average BDR of legitimate users by 20%, while the CPR-CRKG defense scheme reduced the BDR to approximately 1\/4 of the level close to the no-attack case<\/strong>: As shown in <strong>Fig. 12<\/strong> , the BDR increases linearly with the RIS modulation frequency until <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">f_r = f_p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>where it saturates, achieving approximately 20% BDR improvement at a typical location about 2 m from Alice;<strong>Fig. 13<\/strong> The spatial heatmap further shows that the attack effect is strongest when the RIS is close to Alice with a small incidence angle, achieving up to approximately 30% BDR improvement.<strong>The convergence curves in Fig. 9<\/strong> The simulation results further demonstrate that under CPR-CRKG protection, the BDR curve of the attacked scheme (dashed line) is approximately one-quarter of the corresponding attack curve (solid line), and at SNR &lt; 15 dB, the BDR of CPR-CRKG is even lower than that of the original CRKG scheme without attack, verifying the dual suppression effect of the defense scheme against both noise and attacks.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-080de2b0 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-57-51.png ,http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-57-51.png 780w, http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-57-51.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-57-51.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1073\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-23_16-57-51\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-e3c2d50d lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udcdd Citation<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<pre class=\"wp-block-code lab-bibtex-code has-small-font-size\"><code>@article{li2024risjamming,\r\n  author    = {Guyue Li and Paul Staat and Haoyu Li and Markus Heinrichs and Christian Zenger and Rainer Kronberger and Harald Elders-Boll and Christof Paar and Aiqun Hu},\r\n  title     = {{RIS}-Jamming: Breaking Key Consistency in Channel Reciprocity-Based Key Generation},\r\n  journal   = {IEEE Transactions on Information Forensics and Security},\r\n  volume    = {19},\r\n  pages     = {5090--5105},\r\n  year      = {2024},\r\n  doi       = {10.1109\/TIFS.2024.3389569}\r\n}\r\n<\/code><\/pre>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity is-style-wide\" style=\"margin-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-layout-flow wp-block-group-is-layout-flow\">\n<div style=\"height:100px\" aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"wp-block-spacer\"><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-content-justification-space-between is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-788841da wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>Channel Reciprocity-based Key Generation (CRKG) exploits the reciprocal randomness of wireless channels to establish shared keys between wireless terminals, and is considered an important scheme to complement existing cryptographic techniques in future wireless networks. This paper proposes a novel attack\u2014the RIS-jamming attack\u2014and demonstrates that an attacker can disrupt channel reciprocity by deploying a malicious RIS, thereby preventing legitimate users from agreeing on the same key. Specifically, this paper elaborates on three implementation approaches for the RIS-jamming attack: employing active non-reciprocal circuits, performing asynchronous time-varying control, and reducing the signal-to-noise ratio. By establishing a quantitative relationship between the key rate and RIS deployment parameters, the attack effect is theoretically analyzed. To resist such attacks, this paper proposes a defense scheme based on multipath separation of wideband signals\u2014Contaminated Path Removal CRKG (CPR-CRKG)\u2014which identifies and removes malicious RIS paths, enabling legitimate users to still generate keys from the remaining paths. Simulation and experimental results corroborate each other: in a joint experiment with real Wi-Fi devices and a self-built RIS prototype, the attack increased the average bit disagreement rate (BDR) of the original key by 20%, while the proposed CPR-CRKG scheme can effectively mitigate the attack impact when the RIS propagation path is separable.<\/p>","protected":false},"author":1,"featured_media":1070,"comment_status":"open","ping_status":"open","sticky":false,"template":"mypost","format":"standard","meta":{"_uag_custom_page_level_css":"","footnotes":""},"categories":[5],"tags":[],"class_list":["post-1068","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","category-research"],"uagb_featured_image_src":{"full":["http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-57-00.png",743,533,false],"thumbnail":["http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-57-00-150x150.png",150,150,true],"medium":["http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-57-00-300x215.png",300,215,true],"medium_large":["http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-57-00.png",743,533,false],"large":["http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-57-00.png",743,533,false],"1536x1536":["http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-57-00.png",743,533,false],"2048x2048":["http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-57-00.png",743,533,false],"trp-custom-language-flag":["http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-57-00.png",18,12,false]},"uagb_author_info":{"display_name":"jiushihema","author_link":"http:\/\/System.Management.Automation.Internal.Host.InternalHost\/en\/author\/jiushihema\/"},"uagb_comment_info":0,"uagb_excerpt":"\u57fa\u4e8e\u4fe1\u9053\u4e92\u6613\u6027\u7684\u5bc6\u94a5\u751f\u62